WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:08.255
It is Monday at the facilities yard, and Marcus has four things on his tablet before the coffee is gone.

2
00:00:08.305 --> 00:00:13.523
A backlog of work orders. A new technician who wants to learn the boiler.

3
00:00:13.573 --> 00:00:17.059
A new work-order system rolling out from central.

4
00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:20.739
And Events, asking for his crew on the quad Friday.

5
00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:24.275
Four asks, and they are not the same kind of ask.

6
00:00:24.325 --> 00:00:30.770
That is the picture this course gives you: being a manager is four jobs you do every week.

7
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:37.842
Job one, get the work done: Marcus assigns the work orders and checks the ladder job before lunch.

8
00:00:37.892 --> 00:00:40.296
Job two, take care of your people:

9
00:00:40.346 --> 00:00:46.790
he crouches beside the new technician at the boiler panel and shows her the gauge himself.

10
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.955
Job three, make things better:

11
00:00:49.005 --> 00:00:54.728
he opens the new system with a colleague and tries it before he has to teach it.

12
00:00:54.778 --> 00:00:58.625
Job four, connect your team to the rest of Vanderbilt:

13
00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:04.903
he walks the quad with the events coordinator and agrees a plan that protects his week.

14
00:01:04.953 --> 00:01:12.769
Every manager does all four. Most weeks need one more than the others, and part of the job is noticing which.

15
00:01:12.819 --> 00:01:16.305
Marcus pins a four-square sheet on the corkboard.

16
00:01:16.355 --> 00:01:20.346
Each Monday he asks: which job does this week need most?

17
00:01:20.396 --> 00:01:25.831
This week: sort your own list into the four jobs, and see where it is heavy.

